## ===========================================================================
## THE WANDERING MERCENARIES — the `hunted` state's pressure model
##
## The sweep (change document, "The sweep", "The sweep's postures"): once Jack
## has heard the voices in the Alley and the story is `hunted`, a pair of
## mercenaries works the market looking for her. They are NOT a chase — the
## chase begins on Teisha's TE20 trade — they are the pressure that makes
## standing still dangerous. Converted from `story.ni:2037-2182`.
##
## The source's machine, in turns, during its `Avoiding Soldiers` scene:
##
##   1. ARRIVAL. Jack waits in a room; once she has waited longer than a rolled
##      1-3 turn timeout, the mercenaries arrive in her room, `oblivious`.
##   2. DWELL. Oblivious mercenaries in her room for two turns warn her; for
##      three, they spot her and become `approaching`.
##   3. CAPTURE. Approaching mercenaries grab her when a countdown runs out;
##      two turns after that the captain arrives and the game ends in death.
##   4. ESCAPE. Moving while they are `approaching` loses them. Moving while
##      they hold her is refused; attacking them breaks the grip and drops
##      them back to `approaching`, and then she can run.
##   5. TEXTURE. Idle oblivious mercenaries have a 1-in-5 chance per turn of
##      an atmosphere line.
##
## What changed in the port, and why:
##
##   * The third posture is `aggressive`, not the source's `grabbing` (David,
##     2026-08-23): it is a standing state the rest of the model reads, and a
##     standing state wants a posture word.
##   * The rolled 1-3 timeout is a per-turn `one chance in 2` roll once Jack
##     has waited a turn (David, 2026-08-23). Chord has no "random number
##     between" assignment; the pacing is the same in expectation, and the
##     roll runs on the seeded stream so tree-document lines are deterministic.
##   * The arrival reaches WHEREVER JACK IS through a trait on the rooms, not a
##     rule on the mercenaries: `move X to <place>` takes a named room and
##     Chord has no "the player's location" destination, so each swept room
##     owns the arrival and names itself as `it`.
##   * The source suppresses the warning and the spotting when "the
##     mercenaries will not recognise us" (woolen cap off, captain not yet
##     wise — `story.ni:2093-2099`). The cap is not wearable yet, so that
##     branch is NOT carried here; it lands with the disguise work. Until
##     then the "looks right at you … don't recognize you" arrival variant
##     is also held back, and only the "haven't seen you yet" line is used.
##   * Escaped mercenaries are not taken off-stage: Chord's `remove` deletes
##     the entity, so they stay in the room where they lost her until the
##     next arrival moves them (see the swept trait's notes).
##   * `kick` has no verb in the port yet; the break-free reaction is on
##     `attacking`. The source's kick-specific line is held with it.
##
## Not here, on purpose: the conspicuous shopper (`story.ni:3395-3420`), the
## stallkeeper talk-refusals in the mercenaries' presence (`story.ni:1875-1879`),
## and the noisy theft rule — each is its own increment against this model.
##
## Every line below is Gentry's, carried from `story.ni:2037-2182`.
## ===========================================================================

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## THE MERCENARIES
##
## Off-stage until the first arrival. `patience` is the source's "have been in
## the location for N turns"; `countdown` is its `grabbing countdown`.
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Clause by clause, in order:
##
##   1. The dwell clock — runs only while they share Jack's room and have not
##      yet spotted her. Warning at two turns, spotted at three.
##   2. The grab — the countdown starts at 1 on spotting, 2 on a break-free;
##      the grab fires the turn it reaches 0, and re-arms the countdown to
##      2, which is now the captain's clock (`story.ni:2120`, "the leader
##      arrives in two turns from now"). The `when it is approaching` guard
##      on the lowering keeps the grab turn from also ticking the new clock.
##   2a. The captain — two turns of being held and it is over. If she breaks
##      free first, the state has left `aggressive` and nothing happens.
##   3. The dwell clock starts over each time they lose her. A room cannot
##      reach another entity's counter (`its` is the only counter path), so
##      the reset lives here, on the turns they are out of her room.
##   4. Texture — the idle lines, one turn in five while they loiter unaware.
##   5. Provoking oblivious mercenaries is refused (`story.ni:2065`).
##   6. Breaking free (`story.ni:2153-2163`) — works whether they are closing
##      in or already holding her; either way they fall back to approaching
##      with a two-turn countdown, which is her window to run. One `after`
##      clause, since a second would mask the first; the `when` guards split
##      the held and the not-yet-held cases. The held branch must run first,
##      because the first line moves them to approaching.
##   7. Pleading is ignored once they have noticed her.
##
## Counters have no `set`, only `raise`/`lower`, and they clamp at their
## bounds — so "reset to 0" is written as `lower … by <its ceiling>`.

create the wandering mercenaries
  a person, plural
  aka mercenaries, mercenary, thugs, thug, soldiers, soldier, bravos, men, pair
  pronouns they
  states, reversible: oblivious, approaching, aggressive
  counter patience starts 0 between 0 and 3
  counter countdown starts 0 between 0 and 2
  carries the sword

  Rough and ugly, with dirty clothes and mean, scarred faces. They carry
  serious-looking swords on their hips.

  on every turn while it is oblivious and it is here
    raise its patience by 1
    phrase merc-warning when its patience is 2
    change it to approaching when its patience is at least 3
    lower its countdown by 2 when its patience is at least 3
    raise its countdown by 1 when its patience is at least 3
    phrase merc-spotted when its patience is at least 3
  end on

  on every turn while it is approaching and it is here
    change it to aggressive when its countdown is 0
    phrase merc-grab when its countdown is 0
    raise its countdown by 2 when its countdown is 0
    lower its countdown by 1 when it is approaching
  end on

  on every turn while it is aggressive
    kill the player merc-captain-arrives when its countdown is 0
    lower its countdown by 1
  end on

  on every turn while it is not here
    lower its patience by 3
  end on

  on every turn while it is oblivious and it is here and one chance in 5
    phrase merc-idle
  end on

  on attacking it while it is oblivious
    refuse merc-dont-provoke
  end on

  after attacking it
    phrase merc-break-free when it is aggressive
    phrase merc-shove-off when it is approaching
    change it to approaching when it is aggressive
    raise its countdown by 2
  end after

  on talking it while it is not oblivious
    refuse merc-ignore-pleas
  end on

create the sword
  aka swords, blade, blades, steel

  Not some decorated dandy's blades, these. Their steel is dull and tarnished,
  honed to a wicked edge. Nothing less than three-foot-long, practical tools
  for stabbing people dead.

  on taking it
    refuse sword-not-for-you
  end on

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## THE SWEPT ROOMS
##
## The fourteen market rooms between the Alley and the centre post carry this
## trait. It owns three things the source put on the scene: the waiting count
## (reset whenever Jack moves), the arrival (into whichever swept room she is
## standing in), and the two movement rules — losing them, and being held.
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

define counter waiting starts 0 between 0 and 5

## Clause by clause:
##
##   1. The waiting count: raised every hunted turn Jack stands in a swept
##      room, zeroed by the `after going` below. Only swept rooms count, so
##      turns in the Alley or up the post never carry in. (`it is here` on a
##      room means the player is in it.)
##   1a. The arrival. The raise above runs first on the same turn, so "at
##      least 2" is what makes Jack stand still for one full turn before the
##      first roll — the source's timeout, as a roll.
##      (A trait's `after entering it` would have been the natural reset for
##      edges from unswept rooms, but on a trait that gerund binds to the
##      stdlib container-entering action, not room arrival — only a room's
##      own block gets the arrival event. Hence the count is owned here.)
##   2. Held: no leaving while they have her (`story.ni:2137`).
##   3. Losing them (`story.ni:2084-2089`, `story.ni:2147-2150`): moving while
##      they are approaching shakes them off and resets the sweep. Moving
##      while they are merely oblivious just leaves them behind — they will
##      find her again on the next roll. The source takes them off-stage
##      here; Chord's `remove` is terminal (the entity is deleted), so the
##      port leaves them standing where they lost her, oblivious again, and
##      the next arrival moves them. If Jack walks back into that room the
##      dwell clock simply starts — a small deviation, recorded in the header.

define trait swept
  on every turn while hunted and it is here
    raise waiting by 1
  end on

  on every turn while hunted and it is here and the wandering mercenaries is not here and waiting is at least 2 and one chance in 2
    move the wandering mercenaries to it
    change the wandering mercenaries to oblivious
    phrase merc-arrival
  end on

  on going it while the wandering mercenaries is aggressive
    refuse merc-held
  end on

  after going it
    lower waiting by 5
    phrase merc-dash-away when the wandering mercenaries is approaching
    change the wandering mercenaries to oblivious
  end after
end trait

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## THE LINES — all Gentry's
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

define phrase merc-arrival
  You notice a couple of the mercenaries pushing through the crowd, heading
  your way. They haven't seen you yet, but they're getting close.
end phrase

define phrase merc-warning
  Those mercenaries are getting uncomfortably close. You'd better get going
  before they notice you!
end phrase

define phrase merc-spotted
  Suddenly one of the mercenaries does a double-take and sees you. 'There he
  is!' he yells, pointing, and both of them start shoving through the crowd
  towards you.
end phrase

define phrase merc-grab
  A rock-hard fist closes around your upper arm. 'Gotcha!' the mercenary
  yells. The other one starts hollering for his companions.
end phrase

define phrase merc-captain-arrives
  The captain soon arrives, followed by two more of his men. One of them grabs
  your other arm as their leader smiles. 'Good work, dims. Got ourselves a nice
  little commission with this one.' And before you can react, he takes a sack
  of black cloth from his belt and jerks it down over your head.

  Everything goes dark. You can't breathe. You struggle, but you can no more
  break the iron grip on your arms than you could uproot a tree. The men
  holding you curse. Dimly, you hear shouts from the crowd — someone
  protesting this rough treatment of a child.

  'Don't worry, m'lady,' laughs the mercenary leader. 'We'll treat the little
  rat nice an' gentle. Like this, see?'

  Something huge and heavy crashes into your head, and the rest of your senses
  are yanked away. Why they wanted you, you never awake to find out.
end phrase

## The source draws this from three ("pull and struggle" / "beat on the
## mercenary's arms with your fists" / "claw the mercenary's arms skin with
## your fingernails"). A refusal phrase renders its variants as the literal
## word "variants" today (GH #304), so only the first is
## carried until that is fixed.

define phrase merc-held
  You pull and struggle, but the man is just too strong.
end phrase

define phrase merc-dash-away
  You dash away into the crowd, weaving between customers and pushing your way
  through the thickest knots of people. The mercenaries pound after you,
  yelling and shoving people out of their way, but they're too big and too
  slow, and you quickly manage to lose them.
end phrase

## The narrow escapes (`story.ni:2165-2168`). The source picks one at random
## and prefixes "Thinking quickly, you"; the kick variant waits on a kick verb.

define phrase merc-break-free, randomly
  Thinking quickly, you elbow the mercenary as hard as you can, aiming below
  the belt.

  'Argh! Oh, you miserable runt—' the mercenary's grip on your arm loosens,
  and you twist away. You're free!
or
  Thinking quickly, you spit at the mercenary's face, and a gob of spittle
  lands right in his eye.

  'Argh! Oh, you miserable runt—' the mercenary's grip on your arm loosens,
  and you twist away. You're free!
or
  Thinking quickly, you scream your head off. People from two stalls away stop
  what they're doing to see what the commotion is, and when the mercenary
  clamps his hand over your mouth, you bite down hard.

  'Argh! Oh, you miserable runt—' the mercenary's grip on your arm loosens,
  and you twist away. You're free!
end phrase

define phrase merc-shove-off, randomly
  Thinking quickly, you elbow the mercenary as hard as you can, aiming below
  the belt.

  'Argh! Oh, you miserable runt—' He stumbles backwards into his companion.
  Now they're both distracted; now's your chance to run!
or
  Thinking quickly, you spit at the mercenary's face, and a gob of spittle
  lands right in his eye.

  'Argh! Oh, you miserable runt—' He stumbles backwards into his companion.
  Now they're both distracted; now's your chance to run!
or
  Thinking quickly, you scream your head off. People from two stalls away stop
  what they're doing to see what the commotion is, and when the mercenary
  clamps his hand over your mouth, you bite down hard.

  'Argh! Oh, you miserable runt—' He stumbles backwards into his companion.
  Now they're both distracted; now's your chance to run!
end phrase

define phrase merc-dont-provoke
  You resist the foolish impulse, reminding yourself that what you want is for
  them to *not* catch you.
end phrase

define phrase merc-ignore-pleas
  The mercenaries both ignore your pleas.
end phrase

define phrase sword-not-for-you
  Even if you did manage to nick one of those swords without getting yourself
  totally killed, you wouldn't know what to do with it once you had it.
end phrase

## The seven idle lines (`story.ni:2077`). The seventh has its own inner
## random ("the other laughs bawdily / scowls / nods / snarls something back");
## Chord randomises whole arms, so it is expanded to four.

define phrase merc-idle, randomly
  The mercenaries peer around aimlessly. They still haven't spotted you, but
  it's only a matter of time.
or
  The mercenaries look bored.
or
  One of the mercenaries spits on the ground.
or
  One of the mercenaries scratches his head and scowls.
or
  The mercenaries glare at a passing shopper.
or
  One of the mercenaries bumps into a passing shopper, and angrily shoves him
  out of his way.
or
  One of the mercenaries mutters something to his companion; the other laughs
  bawdily.
or
  One of the mercenaries mutters something to his companion; the other scowls.
or
  One of the mercenaries mutters something to his companion; the other nods.
or
  One of the mercenaries mutters something to his companion; the other snarls
  something back.
end phrase
